{"iter": 0, "reply": "# FILE: spend/__init__.py\n```python\n\"\"\"spend \u2014 a small but real personal-expense-tracker CLI.\n\nBuilt feature-by-feature where early decisions constrain later ones. The\nload-bearing decision is the storage schema (see ``spend.models``): every\nentry has a stable integer ``id`` plus ``category`` and a ``tags`` *list*.\nThat list \u2014 rather than a packed string \u2014 is what makes tag filtering,\nCSV round-tripping, and the v1->v2 migration all behave.\n\nPublic surface (kept intentionally small and conventional):\n\n    from spend import Entry, Tracker, Config\n    from spend import load_config, save_config\n    from spend import export_csv, migrate\n    from spend.store import load, save\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom .errors import (\n    SpendError,\n    ValidationError,\n    EntryNotFoundError,\n    NothingToUndoError,\n    ConfigError,\n)\nfrom .models import Entry, SCHEMA_VERSION, parse_date\nfrom .config import Config, load_config, save_config, default_config_path, default_data_path\nfrom .migrations import migrate\nfrom .tracker import Tracker\nfrom .csv_export import export_csv, read_csv, TAG_SEPARATOR\nfrom . import store\n\n__version__ = \"1.0.0\"\n\n__all__ = [\n    \"Entry\",\n    \"Tracker\",\n    \"Config\",\n    \"SCHEMA_VERSION\",\n    \"parse_date\",\n    \"load_config\",\n    \"save_config\",\n    \"default_config_path\",\n    \"default_data_path\",\n    \"migrate\",\n    \"export_csv\",\n    \"read_csv\",\n    \"TAG_SEPARATOR\",\n    \"store\",\n    \"SpendError\",\n    \"ValidationError\",\n    \"EntryNotFoundError\",\n    \"NothingToUndoError\",\n    \"ConfigError\",\n    \"__version__\",\n]\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/errors.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Exception hierarchy for the spend package.\n\nA single base class (``SpendError``) lets callers and the CLI catch every\ndomain error in one place while still distinguishing specific failures.\n\"\"\"\n\n\nclass SpendError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Base class for all errors raised by this package.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass ValidationError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"An entry, config value, or argument failed validation.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass EntryNotFoundError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"No entry with the requested id exists.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass NothingToUndoError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"``undo`` was called but the undo stack is empty.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass ConfigError(SpendError):\n    \"\"\"The config file could not be read or contained bad values.\"\"\"\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/models.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 1 \u2014 the load-bearing decision: the data model / storage schema.\n\nAn :class:`Entry` is the atomic record. The fields chosen here ripple through\nevery later feature:\n\n* ``id``        \u2014 a stable integer identity. *Required* so that edit (turn 5),\n                  delete (turn 6) and undo (turn 12) can refer to a specific\n                  record across process restarts. Never reused.\n* ``amount``    \u2014 a float (money). Summary stats (turn 9) round to cents.\n* ``description`` \u2014 free text.\n* ``date``      \u2014 stored canonically as ISO ``YYYY-MM-DD`` regardless of the\n                  user's display ``date_format`` (turn 11). Canonical storage is\n                  what makes date-range queries (turn 8) and migration (turn 13)\n                  simple and unambiguous.\n* ``category``  \u2014 single string (turn 7). Defaults via config (turn 11).\n* ``tags``      \u2014 a *list* of strings (turn 7).\n\nWhy ``tags`` is a list and not a packed ``\"a,b\"`` string is the crux of turn 15:\na single packed string can't represent a tag that itself contains the packing\ndelimiter, and it forces every consumer (filter, CSV export, migration) to agree\non splitting rules. A real list defers the serialization choice to the boundary\n(the CSV writer) where it can be quoted correctly.\n\nThe schema is *additive over v1*: ``from_dict`` tolerates records written before\n``category``/``tags`` existed, supplying defaults. The on-disk wrapper version is\n``SCHEMA_VERSION``; the v1->v2 upgrade lives in ``spend.migrations``.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport datetime\nimport math\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List\n\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\n\n# On-disk container format version. v1 was a bare JSON list of entry dicts with\n# no category/tags; v2 is a dict wrapper carrying next_id + undo journal.\nSCHEMA_VERSION = 2\n\nDEFAULT_CATEGORY = \"uncategorized\"\n\n\ndef today_iso() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return today's date as an ISO ``YYYY-MM-DD`` string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.date.today().isoformat()\n\n\ndef normalize_date(value: Any) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Coerce ``value`` to a canonical ISO date string, validating it.\n\n    Accepts a ``datetime.date``/``datetime.datetime`` or an ISO string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(value, datetime.datetime):\n        return value.date().isoformat()\n    if isinstance(value, datetime.date):\n        return value.isoformat()\n    s = str(value)\n    try:\n        datetime.date.fromisoformat(s)\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValidationError(f\"date must be ISO YYYY-MM-DD, got {value!r}\") from exc\n    return s\n\n\ndef parse_date(value: str, fmt: str = \"%Y-%m-%d\") -> str:\n    \"\"\"Parse a user-supplied date in ``fmt`` (or ISO) into a canonical ISO string.\n\n    Used by the CLI (turn 11): the user may type dates in their configured\n    ``date_format``, but storage stays ISO.\n    \"\"\"\n    value = str(value).strip()\n    # ISO is always accepted regardless of the configured display format.\n    try:\n        return datetime.date.fromisoformat(value).isoformat()\n    except ValueError:\n        pass\n    try:\n        return datetime.datetime.strptime(value, fmt).date().isoformat()\n    except ValueError as exc:\n        raise ValidationError(\n            f\"could not parse date {value!r} with format {fmt!r} or ISO\"\n        ) from exc\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Entry:\n    \"\"\"A single tracked expense.\"\"\"\n\n    id: int\n    amount: float\n    description: str = \"\"\n    date: str = field(default_factory=today_iso)\n    category: str = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    tags: List[str] = field(default_factory=list)\n\n    def validate(self) -> \"Entry\":\n        \"\"\"Raise :class:`ValidationError` if the entry is malformed.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(self.id, int):\n            raise ValidationError(f\"id must be an int, got {self.id!r}\")\n        try:\n            amount = float(self.amount)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n            raise ValidationError(f\"amount must be numeric, got {self.amount!r}\") from exc\n        if not math.isfinite(amount):\n            raise ValidationError(\"amount must be finite\")\n        self.amount = amount\n        self.date = normalize_date(self.date)\n        if not isinstance(self.tags, list):\n            raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n        self.tags = [str(t) for t in self.tags]\n        self.category = str(self.category) if self.category else DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n        self.description = \"\" if self.description is None else str(self.description)\n        return self\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        \"\"\"Serialize to a JSON-safe dict with a stable field order.\"\"\"\n        return {\n            \"id\": self.id,\n            \"date\": self.date,\n            \"amount\": self.amount,\n            \"category\": self.category,\n            \"tags\": list(self.tags),\n            \"description\": self.description,\n        }\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_dict(cls, data: Dict[str, Any]) -> \"Entry\":\n        \"\"\"Build an Entry from a stored dict.\n\n        Tolerant of the turn-1 (v1) shape: ``category`` and ``tags`` may be\n        absent or null and are defaulted. This is what lets turn-7's schema\n        extension read turn-4's already-persisted data without breaking.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(data, dict):\n            raise ValidationError(f\"entry record must be a dict, got {type(data)!r}\")\n\n        raw_amount = data.get(\"amount\", 0.0)\n        try:\n            amount = float(raw_amount)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n            raise ValidationError(f\"amount must be numeric, got {raw_amount!r}\") from exc\n\n        category = data.get(\"category\")\n        if not category:\n            category = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n        tags = data.get(\"tags\")\n        if tags is None:\n            tags = []\n        if not isinstance(tags, (list, tuple)):\n            raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n\n        date = data.get(\"date\")\n        if date is None:\n            date = today_iso()\n\n        return cls(\n            id=int(data[\"id\"]) if data.get(\"id\") is not None else 0,\n            amount=amount,\n            description=str(data.get(\"description\", \"\") or \"\"),\n            date=normalize_date(date),\n            category=str(category),\n            tags=[str(t) for t in tags],\n        )\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/migrations.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 13 \u2014 migration that upgrades turn-1 (v1) data to the current schema.\n\nTwo historical on-disk shapes must be accepted:\n\n* **v1** (turns 1-6): the file was a *bare JSON list* of entry dicts, with no\n  ``category``/``tags`` and no top-level ``next_id`` or ``undo`` journal.\n* **v2** (turn 7 onward): a dict wrapper\n  ``{\"schema_version\", \"next_id\", \"entries\": [...], \"undo\": [...]}``.\n\n``migrate`` is a pure function over already-parsed JSON. It is idempotent: a v2\ndocument passes through unchanged (aside from filling defaults). The important\ncorrectness point for turn 13 is reconstructing ``next_id`` for v1 data \u2014 v1\nnever stored it, so we derive ``max(id) + 1`` to guarantee future ids don't\ncollide with existing ones.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List\n\nfrom .errors import ValidationError\nfrom .models import SCHEMA_VERSION, DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n\ndef _normalize_entry(record: Dict[str, Any]) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Bring a single stored entry dict up to the v2 field set (additively).\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(record, dict):\n        raise ValidationError(f\"entry record must be a dict, got {type(record)!r}\")\n    out = dict(record)\n    if not out.get(\"category\"):\n        out[\"category\"] = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    if out.get(\"tags\") is None:\n        out[\"tags\"] = []\n    if not isinstance(out[\"tags\"], (list, tuple)):\n        raise ValidationError(\"tags must be a list\")\n    out[\"tags\"] = [str(t) for t in out[\"tags\"]]\n    if \"description\" not in out or out[\"description\"] is None:\n        out[\"description\"] = \"\"\n    return out\n\n\ndef migrate(raw: Any) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Return a canonical v2 document from any supported historical shape.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(raw, list):\n        # v1: a bare list of entries.\n        entries: List[Any] = raw\n        next_id = None\n        undo: List[Any] = []\n    elif isinstance(raw, dict):\n        entries = raw.get(\"entries\", [])\n        next_id = raw.get(\"next_id\")\n        undo = raw.get(\"undo\", []) or []\n        if not isinstance(entries, list):\n            raise ValidationError(\"'entries' must be a list\")\n    else:\n        raise ValidationError(f\"unrecognized data file shape: {type(raw)!r}\")\n\n    normalized: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []\n    max_id = 0\n    for index, record in enumerate(entries):\n        rec = _normalize_entry(record)\n        # v1 records should always have had ids, but assign defensively so a\n        # hand-edited file never produces colliding/zero ids.\n        rec_id = rec.get(\"id\")\n        if not isinstance(rec_id, int) or rec_id <= 0:\n            rec_id = max_id + 1 if max_id else index + 1\n            rec[\"id\"] = rec_id\n        else:\n            rec[\"id\"] = int(rec_id)\n        normalized.append(rec)\n        max_id = max(max_id, rec[\"id\"])\n\n    if not isinstance(next_id, int) or next_id <= max_id:\n        next_id = max_id + 1\n\n    return {\n        \"schema_version\": SCHEMA_VERSION,\n        \"next_id\": next_id,\n        \"entries\": normalized,\n        \"undo\": list(undo),\n    }\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/store.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 4 \u2014 persistence to disk, honoring the turn-1 schema.\n\nOn load we always route the parsed JSON through ``migrate`` (turn 13), so the\nrest of the program only ever sees the current schema. Writes are atomic\n(write-temp-then-rename) so a crash mid-write can't corrupt the data file.\n\nThe undo journal (turn 12) is persisted *inside* the same document so that\n\"undo last action\" survives between CLI invocations \u2014 without that, undo would\nbe useless in a process-per-command CLI.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List\n\nfrom .models import Entry, SCHEMA_VERSION\nfrom .migrations import migrate\n\n\ndef load(path: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Load a data file, migrating as needed.\n\n    Returns a dict ``{\"schema_version\", \"next_id\", \"entries\": [Entry...],\n    \"undo\": [...]}``. A missing file yields an empty database.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not path or not os.path.exists(path):\n        return {\"schema_version\": SCHEMA_VERSION, \"next_id\": 1, \"entries\": [], \"undo\": []}\n\n    with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n        raw = json.load(handle)\n\n    data = migrate(raw)\n    entries: List[Entry] = [Entry.from_dict(rec) for rec in data[\"entries\"]]\n    return {\n        \"schema_version\": data[\"schema_version\"],\n        \"next_id\": data[\"next_id\"],\n        \"entries\": entries,\n        \"undo\": data.get(\"undo\", []),\n    }\n\n\ndef save(path: str, *, next_id: int, entries: List[Entry], undo: List[Any] = None) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Atomically write the database to ``path`` in the current schema.\"\"\"\n    payload = {\n        \"schema_version\": SCHEMA_VERSION,\n        \"next_id\": int(next_id),\n        \"entries\": [e.to_dict() for e in entries],\n        \"undo\": list(undo or []),\n    }\n    parent = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path))\n    if parent:\n        os.makedirs(parent, exist_ok=True)\n    tmp = path + \".tmp\"\n    with open(tmp, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n        json.dump(payload, handle, indent=2, sort_keys=False)\n        handle.write(\"\\n\")\n    os.replace(tmp, path)\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/tracker.py\n```python\n\"\"\"The in-memory model and every mutating/querying operation.\n\nThis module is storage-agnostic on purpose \u2014 the CLI wires it to ``store``.\nIt owns:\n\n* turn 2  add\n* turn 3  list / filter\n* turn 5  edit\n* turn 6  delete\n* turn 8  date-range queries\n* turn 9  summary stats\n* turn 12 undo\n\nUndo model (turn 12): every mutating operation pushes a *typed inverse* onto an\nundo journal \u2014 not a full snapshot. This is the explicit understanding of the\nturn-5/6 mutation model:\n\n    add    -> (\"add\",  id)                 inverse: remove that id\n    edit   -> (\"edit\", before-dict)        inverse: restore prior field values\n    delete -> (\"delete\", index, dict)      inverse: re-insert at original index\n\nLIFO ordering means an edit-after-add is undone before the add, which matches\nuser intuition for \"undo last action\". The journal is serialized to disk by the\nstore so undo works across CLI invocations.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport datetime\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional\n\nfrom .errors import EntryNotFoundError, NothingToUndoError, ValidationError\nfrom .models import Entry, normalize_date, today_iso, DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n\nclass _Cfg:\n    \"\"\"Tiny duck-typed config holder used when no Config is supplied.\"\"\"\n\n    default_category = DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n    date_format = \"%Y-%m-%d\"\n\n\ndef _round_money(value: float) -> float:\n    return round(float(value), 2)\n\n\nclass Tracker:\n    \"\"\"Holds entries and applies operations against them.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        entries: Optional[List[Entry]] = None,\n        next_id: int = 1,\n        undo: Optional[List[Any]] = None,\n        config: Any = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        self.entries: List[Entry] = list(entries or [])\n        self.next_id: int = int(next_id)\n        self.undo_stack: List[Dict[str, Any]] = list(undo or [])\n        self.config = config if config is not None else _Cfg()\n\n    # -- helpers ---------------------------------------------------------\n    def _index_of(self, entry_id: int) -> int:\n        for i, e in enumerate(self.entries):\n            if e.id == entry_id:\n                return i\n        raise EntryNotFoundError(f\"no entry with id {entry_id}\")\n\n    def get(self, entry_id: int) -> Entry:\n        return self.entries[self._index_of(entry_id)]\n\n    def _push_undo(self, record: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n        self.undo_stack.append(record)\n\n    def _default_category(self) -> str:\n        return getattr(self.config, \"default_category\", DEFAULT_CATEGORY) or DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n\n    # -- turn 2: add -----------------------------------------------------\n    def add(\n        self,\n        amount: float,\n        description: str = \"\",\n        date: Any = None,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n        tags: Optional[List[str]] = None,\n    ) -> Entry:\n        entry = Entry(\n            id=self.next_id,\n            amount=float(amount),\n            description=description or \"\",\n            date=normalize_date(date) if date else today_iso(),\n            category=category or self._default_category(),\n            tags=[str(t) for t in (tags or [])],\n        )\n        entry.validate()\n        self.entries.append(entry)\n        self.next_id += 1\n        self._push_undo({\"op\": \"add\", \"id\": entry.id})\n        return entry\n\n    # -- turn 5: edit ----------------------------------------------------\n    def edit(self, entry_id: int, **changes: Any) -> Entry:\n        entry = self.get(entry_id)\n        before = entry.to_dict()\n        allowed = {\"amount\", \"description\", \"date\", \"category\", \"tags\"}\n        applied = False\n        for key, value in changes.items():\n            if value is None:\n                continue\n            if key not in allowed:\n                raise ValidationError(f\"cannot edit field {key!r}\")\n            if key == \"amount\":\n                value = float(value)\n            elif key == \"date\":\n                value = normalize_date(value)\n            elif key == \"tags\":\n                value = [str(t) for t in value]\n            elif key == \"category\":\n                value = str(value) or DEFAULT_CATEGORY\n            else:\n                value = str(value)\n            setattr(entry, key, value)\n            applied = True\n        entry.validate()\n        if applied:\n            self._push_undo({\"op\": \"edit\", \"before\": before})\n        return entry\n\n    # -- turn 6: delete --------------------------------------------------\n    def delete(self, entry_id: int) -> Entry:\n        index = self._index_of(entry_id)\n        entry = self.entries.pop(index)\n        self._push_undo({\"op\": \"delete\", \"index\": index, \"entry\": entry.to_dict()})\n        return entry\n\n    # -- turn 12: undo ---------------------------------------------------\n    def undo(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        if not self.undo_stack:\n            raise NothingToUndoError(\"nothing to undo\")\n        record = self.undo_stack.pop()\n        op = record[\"op\"]\n        if op == \"add\":\n            index = self._index_of(record[\"id\"])\n            self.entries.pop(index)\n        elif op == \"edit\":\n            before = record[\"before\"]\n            index = self._index_of(before[\"id\"])\n            self.entries[index] = Entry.from_dict(before)\n        elif op == \"delete\":\n            restored = Entry.from_dict(record[\"entry\"])\n            index = min(record.get(\"index\", len(self.entries)), len(self.entries))\n            self.entries.insert(index, restored)\n        else:  # pragma: no cover - defensive\n            raise ValidationError(f\"unknown undo op {op!r}\")\n        return record\n\n    # -- turn 8: date-range ---------------------------------------------\n    @staticmethod\n    def _within(entry: Entry, start: Optional[str], end: Optional[str]) -> bool:\n        d = datetime.date.fromisoformat(entry.date)\n        if start is not None and d < datetime.date.fromisoformat(normalize_date(start)):\n            return False\n        if end is not None and d > datetime.date.fromisoformat(normalize_date(end)):\n            return False\n        return True\n\n    def entries_between(self, start: Optional[str], end: Optional[str]) -> List[Entry]:\n        return self.list(start=start, end=end)\n\n    # -- turn 3: list / filter ------------------------------------------\n    def list(\n        self,\n        category: Optional[str] = None,\n        tag: Optional[str] = None,\n        text: Optional[str] = None,\n        start: Optional[str] = None,\n        end: Optional[str] = None,\n    ) -> List[Entry]:\n        result = list(self.entries)\n        if category is not None:\n            result = [e for e in result if e.category == category]\n        if tag is not None:\n            # Membership against the real list \u2014 the whole point of turn 7.\n            result = [e for e in result if tag in e.tags]\n        if text is not None:\n            needle = text.lower()\n            result = [e for e in result if needle in e.description.lower()]\n        if start is not None or end is not None:\n            result = [e for e in result if self._within(e, start, end)]\n        return sorted(result, key=lambda e: (e.date, e.id))\n\n    # Convenience alias.\n    def filter(self, **kwargs: Any) -> List[Entry]:\n        return self.list(**kwargs)\n\n    # -- turn 9: summary stats ------------------------------------------\n    def summary(self, **filters: Any) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        entries = self.list(**filters)\n        count = len(entries)\n        total = _round_money(sum(e.amount for e in entries)) if entries else 0.0\n\n        by_category: Dict[str, float] = {}\n        by_tag: Dict[str, float] = {}\n        for e in entries:\n            by_category[e.category] = _round_money(by_category.get(e.category, 0.0) + e.amount)\n            for t in e.tags:\n                by_tag[t] = _round_money(by_tag.get(t, 0.0) + e.amount)\n\n        dates = sorted(e.date for e in entries)\n        amounts = [e.amount for e in entries]\n        return {\n            \"count\": count,\n            \"total\": total,\n            \"average\": _round_money(total / count) if count else 0.0,\n            \"min\": _round_money(min(amounts)) if amounts else 0.0,\n            \"max\": _round_money(max(amounts)) if amounts else 0.0,\n            \"first_date\": dates[0] if dates else None,\n            \"last_date\": dates[-1] if dates else None,\n            \"by_category\": by_category,\n            \"by_tag\": by_tag,\n        }\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/config.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 11 \u2014 a config file holding user preferences.\n\nTwo preferences are stored: ``default_category`` (used by ``add`` when none is\ngiven) and ``date_format`` (how the user types/sees dates; storage stays ISO).\n``data_path`` is also persisted so the user can point the config at their data\nfile once.\n\nConfig lives in its own JSON file, separate from the data file, so editing\npreferences never risks the expense data.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, asdict\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, Optional\n\nfrom .errors import ConfigError\n\n\ndef _spend_home() -> str:\n    return os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(\"~\"), \".spend\")\n\n\ndef default_config_path() -> str:\n    return os.environ.get(\"SPEND_CONFIG\") or os.path.join(_spend_home(), \"config.json\")\n\n\ndef default_data_path() -> str:\n    return os.environ.get(\"SPEND_DATA\") or os.path.join(_spend_home(), \"data.json\")\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Config:\n    default_category: str = \"uncategorized\"\n    date_format: str = \"%Y-%m-%d\"\n    data_path: Optional[str] = None\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        return asdict(self)\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_dict(cls, data: Dict[str, Any]) -> \"Config\":\n        if not isinstance(data, dict):\n            raise ConfigError(\"config file must contain a JSON object\")\n        known = {f for f in (\"default_category\", \"date_format\", \"data_path\")}\n        kwargs = {k: v for k, v in data.items() if k in known}\n        return cls(**kwargs)\n\n    def resolved_data_path(self) -> str:\n        return self.data_path or default_data_path()\n\n\ndef load_config(path: Optional[str] = None) -> Config:\n    \"\"\"Load config from ``path`` (or the default). Missing file -> defaults.\"\"\"\n    path = path or default_config_path()\n    if not os.path.exists(path):\n        return Config()\n    try:\n        with open(path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n            data = json.load(handle)\n    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:\n        raise ConfigError(f\"could not read config at {path}: {exc}\") from exc\n    return Config.from_dict(data)\n\n\ndef save_config(config: Config, path: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Persist ``config`` to ``path`` (or the default). Returns the path used.\"\"\"\n    path = path or default_config_path()\n    parent = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path))\n    if parent:\n        os.makedirs(parent, exist_ok=True)\n    with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n        json.dump(config.to_dict(), handle, indent=2)\n        handle.write(\"\\n\")\n    return path\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/csv_export.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 10 \u2014 export to CSV, serializing turn-7 tags correctly.\nTurn 15 \u2014 the bugfix lives here, and depends on *why* tags are a list.\n\nThe naive first cut joined tags with a comma and hand-built CSV rows. That has\ntwo failure modes:\n\n1. A comma is the CSV field separator, so ``\",\".join(tags)`` written into a\n   raw line silently created extra columns.\n2. Even with the ``csv`` module, using a comma as the *intra-field* tag\n   separator makes a tag like ``\"food,drinks\"`` indistinguishable from two\n   tags ``\"food\"`` and ``\"drinks\"`` on read-back.\n\nThe fix \u2014 and the reason turn 7 modeled tags as a real list rather than a\npre-joined string \u2014 is to defer serialization to this boundary: write through\n``csv.writer`` (which quotes fields containing the delimiter) and join tags with\na dedicated, non-comma ``TAG_SEPARATOR`` so they round-trip unambiguously.\n``read_csv`` is the inverse and exists to prove the round trip in tests.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport csv\nimport io\nfrom typing import Any, Iterable, List, Union\n\nfrom .models import Entry\n\n# Intra-field tag delimiter. Deliberately NOT a comma (the CSV field separator).\nTAG_SEPARATOR = \";\"\n\nFIELDS = [\"id\", \"date\", \"amount\", \"category\", \"tags\", \"description\"]\n\n\ndef _join_tags(tags: Iterable[str]) -> str:\n    return TAG_SEPARATOR.join(str(t) for t in tags)\n\n\ndef _split_tags(value: str) -> List[str]:\n    if not value:\n        return []\n    return [t for t in value.split(TAG_SEPARATOR) if t != \"\"]\n\n\ndef export_csv(entries: Iterable[Entry], target: Union[str, Any]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Write ``entries`` as CSV to a file path or an open text file object.\"\"\"\n    own_handle = False\n    if isinstance(target, str):\n        handle = open(target, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\")\n        own_handle = True\n    else:\n        handle = target\n    try:\n        writer = csv.writer(handle)\n        writer.writerow(FIELDS)\n        for e in entries:\n            writer.writerow(\n                [\n                    e.id,\n                    e.date,\n                    f\"{e.amount:.2f}\",\n                    e.category,\n                    _join_tags(e.tags),\n                    e.description,\n                ]\n            )\n    finally:\n        if own_handle:\n            handle.close()\n\n\ndef export_csv_string(entries: Iterable[Entry]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the CSV serialization as a string (convenience for the CLI).\"\"\"\n    buffer = io.StringIO()\n    export_csv(entries, buffer)\n    return buffer.getvalue()\n\n\ndef read_csv(target: Union[str, Any]) -> List[Entry]:\n    \"\"\"Inverse of :func:`export_csv`. Proves tags round-trip correctly.\"\"\"\n    own_handle = False\n    if isinstance(target, str):\n        handle = open(target, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\")\n        own_handle = True\n    else:\n        handle = target\n    try:\n        reader = csv.DictReader(handle)\n        out: List[Entry] = []\n        for row in reader:\n            out.append(\n                Entry.from_dict(\n                    {\n                        \"id\": int(row[\"id\"]) if row.get(\"id\") else 0,\n                        \"date\": row.get(\"date\"),\n                        \"amount\": float(row.get(\"amount\", 0) or 0),\n                        \"category\": row.get(\"category\"),\n                        \"tags\": _split_tags(row.get(\"tags\", \"\")),\n                        \"description\": row.get(\"description\", \"\"),\n                    }\n                )\n            )\n        return out\n    finally:\n        if own_handle:\n            handle.close()\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/cli.py\n```python\n\"\"\"The argparse-based command line interface, wiring tracker <-> store <-> config.\n\nEach invocation: load config -> load (and implicitly migrate) the data file ->\nrun one subcommand -> persist. Subcommands map to the feature turns:\n\n    add (2), list (3), edit (5), delete (6, with confirmation), range (8),\n    summary (9), export (10), config (11), undo (12), migrate (13).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\nfrom . import store\nfrom .config import Config, load_config, save_config, default_config_path\nfrom .csv_export import export_csv, export_csv_string\nfrom .errors import SpendError\nfrom .models import parse_date\nfrom .tracker import Tracker\n\n\ndef build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\"spend\", description=\"Personal expense tracker.\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--data\", help=\"path to the data file (overrides config)\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--config\", help=\"path to the config file\")\n    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\", required=True)\n\n    p_add = sub.add_parser(\"add\", help=\"add an expense entry\")\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--amount\", type=float, required=True)\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--description\", \"--desc\", default=\"\")\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--date\", default=None)\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    p_add.add_argument(\"--tag\", action=\"append\", dest=\"tags\", default=None)\n\n    p_list = sub.add_parser(\"list\", help=\"list/filter entries\")\n    p_list.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    p_list.add_argument(\"--tag\", default=None)\n    p_list.add_argument(\"--text\", default=None)\n    p_list.add_argument(\"--start\", default=None)\n    p_list.add_argument(\"--end\", default=None)\n\n    p_edit = sub.add_parser(\"edit\", help=\"edit an entry\")\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"id\", type=int)\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--amount\", type=float, default=None)\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--description\", \"--desc\", default=None)\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--date\", default=None)\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--tags\", default=None, help=\"comma-separated, replaces tags\")\n\n    p_del = sub.add_parser(\"delete\", help=\"delete an entry (asks to confirm)\")\n    p_del.add_argument(\"id\", type=int)\n    p_del.add_argument(\"--yes\", \"-y\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"skip confirmation\")\n\n    p_range = sub.add_parser(\"range\", help=\"entries within a date range\")\n    p_range.add_argument(\"--start\", default=None)\n    p_range.add_argument(\"--end\", default=None)\n\n    p_sum = sub.add_parser(\"summary\", help=\"summary statistics\")\n    p_sum.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    p_sum.add_argument(\"--tag\", default=None)\n    p_sum.add_argument(\"--start\", default=None)\n    p_sum.add_argument(\"--end\", default=None)\n\n    p_exp = sub.add_parser(\"export\", help=\"export to CSV\")\n    p_exp.add_argument(\"--output\", \"-o\", default=None, help=\"output path (default stdout)\")\n    p_exp.add_argument(\"--category\", default=None)\n    p_exp.add_argument(\"--tag\", default=None)\n    p_exp.add_argument(\"--start\", default=None)\n    p_exp.add_argument(\"--end\", default=None)\n\n    p_cfg = sub.add_parser(\"config\", help=\"view or change configuration\")\n    cfg_sub = p_cfg.add_subparsers(dest=\"config_action\", required=True)\n    cfg_sub.add_parser(\"show\")\n    p_cfg_set = cfg_sub.add_parser(\"set\")\n    p_cfg_set.add_argument(\"key\")\n    p_cfg_set.add_argument(\"value\")\n\n    sub.add_parser(\"undo\", help=\"undo the last add/edit/delete\")\n    sub.add_parser(\"migrate\", help=\"rewrite the data file in the current schema\")\n    return parser\n\n\ndef _open(args) -> tuple:\n    config_path = args.config or default_config_path()\n    config = load_config(config_path)\n    data_path = args.data or config.resolved_data_path()\n    db = store.load(data_path)\n    tracker = Tracker(\n        entries=db[\"entries\"], next_id=db[\"next_id\"], undo=db[\"undo\"], config=config\n    )\n    return config, config_path, data_path, tracker\n\n\ndef _persist(data_path: str, tracker: Tracker) -> None:\n    store.save(\n        data_path,\n        next_id=tracker.next_id,\n        entries=tracker.entries,\n        undo=tracker.undo_stack,\n    )\n\n\ndef _fmt(entry, out) -> None:\n    tags = \",\".join(entry.tags)\n    out.write(\n        f\"#{entry.id} {entry.date} {entry.amount:.2f} [{entry.category}]\"\n        f\"{(' {' + tags + '}') if tags else ''} {entry.description}\\n\"\n    )\n\n\ndef main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:\n    parser = build_parser()\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n    out = sys.stdout\n    err = sys.stderr\n\n    try:\n        config, config_path, data_path, tracker = _open(args)\n        cmd = args.command\n\n        if cmd == \"add\":\n            date = parse_date(args.date, config.date_format) if args.date else None\n            entry = tracker.add(\n                amount=args.amount,\n                description=args.description,\n                date=date,\n                category=args.category,\n                tags=args.tags,\n            )\n            _persist(data_path, tracker)\n            out.write(f\"added #{entry.id}\\n\")\n\n        elif cmd == \"list\":\n            for e in tracker.list(\n                category=args.category,\n                tag=args.tag,\n                text=args.text,\n                start=_d(args.start, config),\n                end=_d(args.end, config),\n            ):\n                _fmt(e, out)\n\n        elif cmd == \"edit\":\n            tags = (\n                [t.strip() for t in args.tags.split(\",\") if t.strip()]\n                if args.tags is not None\n                else None\n            )\n            date = parse_date(args.date, config.date_format) if args.date else None\n            tracker.edit(\n                args.id,\n                amount=args.amount,\n                description=args.description,\n                date=date,\n                category=args.category,\n                tags=tags,\n            )\n            _persist(data_path, tracker)\n            out.write(f\"edited #{args.id}\\n\")\n\n        elif cmd == \"delete\":\n            entry = tracker.get(args.id)\n            if not args.yes:\n                _fmt(entry, out)\n                answer = input(f\"delete #{args.id}? [y/N] \").strip().lower()\n                if answer not in (\"y\", \"yes\"):\n                    out.write(\"aborted\\n\")\n                    return 0\n            tracker.delete(args.id)\n            _persist(data_path, tracker)\n            out.write(f\"deleted #{args.id}\\n\")\n\n        elif cmd == \"range\":\n            for e in tracker.entries_between(_d(args.start, config), _d(args.end, config)):\n                _fmt(e, out)\n\n        elif cmd == \"summary\":\n            stats = tracker.summary(\n                category=args.category,\n                tag=args.tag,\n                start=_d(args.start, config),\n                end=_d(args.end, config),\n            )\n            out.write(json.dumps(stats, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n\n        elif cmd == \"export\":\n            entries = tracker.list(\n                category=args.category,\n                tag=args.tag,\n                start=_d(args.start, config),\n                end=_d(args.end, config),\n            )\n            if args.output:\n                export_csv(entries, args.output)\n                out.write(f\"exported {len(entries)} entries to {args.output}\\n\")\n            else:\n                out.write(export_csv_string(entries))\n\n        elif cmd == \"config\":\n            if args.config_action == \"show\":\n                out.write(json.dumps(config.to_dict(), indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n            elif args.config_action == \"set\":\n                if args.key not in (\"default_category\", \"date_format\", \"data_path\"):\n                    err.write(f\"unknown config key: {args.key}\\n\")\n                    return 2\n                setattr(config, args.key, args.value)\n                save_config(config, config_path)\n                out.write(f\"set {args.key} = {args.value}\\n\")\n\n        elif cmd == \"undo\":\n            record = tracker.undo()\n            _persist(data_path, tracker)\n            out.write(f\"undid {record['op']}\\n\")\n\n        elif cmd == \"migrate\":\n            # load() already migrated in-memory; persisting writes current schema.\n            _persist(data_path, tracker)\n            out.write(f\"migrated {data_path} to current schema\\n\")\n\n        else:  # pragma: no cover - argparse guards this\n            parser.error(f\"unknown command {cmd!r}\")\n\n    except SpendError as exc:\n        err.write(f\"error: {exc}\\n\")\n        return 1\n    return 0\n\n\ndef _d(value, config):\n    \"\"\"Parse an optional CLI date in the configured format to ISO, or None.\"\"\"\n    if value is None:\n        return None\n    return parse_date(value, config.date_format)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":  # pragma: no cover\n    sys.exit(main())\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/__main__.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Allow ``python -m spend``.\"\"\"\n\nimport sys\n\nfrom .cli import main\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/tests/__init__.py\n```python\n```\n\n# FILE: spend/tests/test_spend.py\n```python\n\"\"\"Turn 14 \u2014 tests across the whole thing.\n\nSelf-contained (stdlib ``unittest`` + ``tempfile``); also collected fine by\npytest. Exercises the model, persistence round-trip, every mutation, undo,\ndate-range, summary, CSV tag round-trip, config, and the v1->v2 migration.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport csv\nimport io\nimport os\nimport tempfile\nimport unittest\n\nfrom spend import store\nfrom spend.config import Config, load_config, save_config\nfrom spend.csv_export import export_csv_string, read_csv, TAG_SEPARATOR\nfrom spend.errors import EntryNotFoundError, NothingToUndoError\nfrom spend.migrations import migrate\nfrom spend.models import Entry, SCHEMA_VERSION, parse_date\nfrom spend.tracker import Tracker\n\n\nclass ModelTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_roundtrip(self):\n        e = Entry(id=1, amount=9.99, description=\"lunch\", date=\"2024-01-02\",\n                  category=\"food\", tags=[\"work\", \"reimbursable\"])\n        self.assertEqual(Entry.from_dict(e.to_dict()), e)\n\n    def test_v1_record_defaults_tags_and_category(self):\n        e = Entry.from_dict({\"id\": 1, \"amount\": 5, \"date\": \"2024-01-01\"})\n        self.assertEqual(e.category, \"uncategorized\")\n        self.assertEqual(e.tags, [])\n\n    def test_parse_date_custom_format(self):\n        self.assertEqual(parse_date(\"02/03/2024\", \"%m/%d/%Y\"), \"2024-02-03\")\n\n\nclass TrackerTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp(self):\n        self.t = Tracker(config=Config(default_category=\"general\"))\n\n    def test_add_uses_default_category(self):\n        e = self.t.add(amount=10, description=\"x\")\n        self.assertEqual(e.id, 1)\n        self.assertEqual(e.category, \"general\")\n\n    def test_filter_by_tag(self):\n        self.t.add(10, \"a\", tags=[\"food\"])\n        self.t.add(20, \"b\", tags=[\"travel\"])\n        self.assertEqual([e.description for e in self.t.list(tag=\"food\")], [\"a\"])\n\n    def test_edit_and_undo(self):\n        e = self.t.add(10, \"a\")\n        self.t.edit(e.id, amount=15)\n        self.assertEqual(self.t.get(e.id).amount, 15)\n        self.t.undo()\n        self.assertEqual(self.t.get(e.id).amount, 10)\n\n    def test_delete_and_undo_restores_position(self):\n        self.t.add(1, \"a\")\n        self.t.add(2, \"b\")\n        self.t.add(3, \"c\")\n        self.t.delete(2)\n        self.assertEqual([e.id for e in self.t.entries], [1, 3])\n        self.t.undo()\n        self.assertEqual([e.id for e in self.t.entries], [1, 2, 3])\n\n    def test_undo_add(self):\n        self.t.add(1, \"a\")\n        self.t.undo()\n        self.assertEqual(self.t.entries, [])\n\n    def test_undo_empty_raises(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(NothingToUndoError):\n            self.t.undo()\n\n    def test_missing_entry_raises(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(EntryNotFoundError):\n            self.t.get(999)\n\n    def test_date_range(self):\n        self.t.add(1, \"a\", date=\"2024-01-01\")\n        self.t.add(2, \"b\", date=\"2024-02-15\")\n        self.t.add(3, \"c\", date=\"2024-03-20\")\n        got = [e.description for e in self.t.entries_between(\"2024-02-01\", \"2024-02-28\")]\n        self.assertEqual(got, [\"b\"])\n\n    def test_summary(self):\n        self.t.add(10, \"a\", category=\"food\", tags=[\"work\"])\n        self.t.add(20, \"b\", category=\"food\", tags=[\"work\", \"trip\"])\n        self.t.add(30, \"c\", category=\"rent\")\n        s = self.t.summary()\n        self.assertEqual(s[\"count\"], 3)\n        self.assertEqual(s[\"total\"], 60.0)\n        self.assertEqual(s[\"by_category\"][\"food\"], 30.0)\n        self.assertEqual(s[\"by_tag\"][\"work\"], 30.0)\n\n\nclass PersistenceTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_round_trip_through_store(self):\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:\n            path = os.path.join(d, \"data.json\")\n            t = Tracker()\n            t.add(12.5, \"coffee\", category=\"food\", tags=[\"x\"])\n            store.save(path, next_id=t.next_id, entries=t.entries, undo=t.undo_stack)\n            db = store.load(path)\n            self.assertEqual(db[\"schema_version\"], SCHEMA_VERSION)\n            self.assertEqual(db[\"entries\"][0].description, \"coffee\")\n            self.assertEqual(db[\"entries\"][0].tags, [\"x\"])\n\n    def test_migrate_v1_bare_list(self):\n        # turn-1/v1 shape: a bare list, no tags/category, no next_id.\n        raw = [\n            {\"id\": 1, \"amount\": 5, \"description\": \"a\", \"date\": \"2024-01-01\"},\n            {\"id\": 2, \"amount\": 6, \"description\": \"b\", \"date\": \"2024-01-02\"},\n        ]\n        data = migrate(raw)\n        self.assertEqual(data[\"schema_version\"], SCHEMA_VERSION)\n        self.assertEqual(data[\"next_id\"], 3)\n        self.assertEqual(data[\"entries\"][0][\"category\"], \"uncategorized\")\n        self.assertEqual(data[\"entries\"][0][\"tags\"], [])\n\n\nclass CsvTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_tags_roundtrip_with_comma_in_tag(self):\n        # The turn-15 reason tags are a list: a tag may contain a comma.\n        t = Tracker()\n        t.add(9.0, \"dinner\", category=\"food\", tags=[\"food,drinks\", \"work\"])\n        text = export_csv_string(t.entries)\n        # The CSV writer must quote the tags field (it contains a comma).\n        rows = list(csv.reader(io.StringIO(text)))\n        self.assertEqual(rows[0], [\"id\", \"date\", \"amount\", \"category\", \"tags\", \"description\"])\n        back = read_csv(io.StringIO(text))\n        self.assertEqual(back[0].tags, [\"food,drinks\", \"work\"])\n        self.assertNotIn(\",\", TAG_SEPARATOR)\n\n\nclass ConfigTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_save_and_load(self):\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:\n            path = os.path.join(d, \"config.json\")\n            save_config(Config(default_category=\"bills\", date_format=\"%d/%m/%Y\"), path)\n            cfg = load_config(path)\n            self.assertEqual(cfg.default_category, \"bills\")\n            self.assertEqual(cfg.date_format, \"%d/%m/%Y\")\n\n    def test_missing_config_returns_defaults(self):\n        cfg = load_config(os.path.join(tempfile.gettempdir(), \"does-not-exist-xyz.json\"))\n        self.assertEqual(cfg.default_category, \"uncategorized\")\n\n\nclass CliTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_add_then_list(self):\n        import contextlib\n        from spend.cli import main\n\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:\n            data = os.path.join(d, \"data.json\")\n            cfg = os.path.join(d, \"config.json\")\n            common = [\"--data\", data, \"--config\", cfg]\n            self.assertEqual(main(common + [\"add\", \"--amount\", \"4.5\", \"--desc\", \"tea\"]), 0)\n            buf = io.StringIO()\n            with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):\n                main(common + [\"list\"])\n            self.assertIn(\"tea\", buf.getvalue())\n            # delete with --yes, then undo.\n            self.assertEqual(main(common + [\"delete\", \"1\", \"--yes\"]), 0)\n            self.assertEqual(main(common + [\"undo\"]), 0)\n            buf2 = io.StringIO()\n            with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf2):\n                main(common + [\"list\"])\n            self.assertIn(\"tea\", buf2.getvalue())\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n```", "summary": "1 error"}